+# R46 B7a — Vulkan cumulative heap reservation accounting
+
+- Worktree: `C:/ArifiLabs/cache/r46b-wt`
+- Branch: `lane-230/r46b-rebased-2026-09-11`
+- Inherited HEAD: `ae831efde` (`test(vulkan): add independent S-X8 route checks`) — unchanged; nothing
+  was fetched, pulled, merged, rebased, committed, pushed or tagged. All work is in the working tree.
+- Date: 2026-09-13
+
+## 1. What was implemented
+
+A thread-safe cumulative per-memory-heap reservation ledger in
+`ggml/src/ggml-vulkan/ggml-vulkan.cpp`, used by every Vulkan buffer allocation (not S-X8-only).
+
+Before this change `ggml_vk_find_memory_properties()` accepted a memory type whenever the heap's
+whole reported size was at least the single `mem_req.size`. N concurrent or sequential allocations
+could each be admitted against the same bytes; the driver then failed on a later allocation or on the
+first pinned upload. That is the defect R47d traced and the one this phase closes.
+
+### Design invariants
+
+| # | Invariant | Where it is enforced |
+|---|---|---|
+| 1 | A reservation is taken **before** `allocateMemory` is asked and before bind/map ownership is accepted | `ggml_vk_create_buffer()` candidate loop, `reservation.try_reserve(...)` |
+| 2 | Cumulative reservations per heap can never exceed the heap budget, under concurrency | `vk_heap_ledger::reserve()` under one `std::mutex` |
+| 3 | Admission arithmetic never forms a sum, so it cannot overflow: `bytes > budget - reserved` | `vk_heap_ledger::reserve()` / `would_fit()` |
+| 4 | A refused candidate, a throwing `allocateMemory`, or a throwing bind/map/BDA setup rolls back exactly its own reservation | `vk_heap_reservation` RAII guard at **function scope** in `ggml_vk_create_buffer()` |
+| 5 | A successful buffer owns exactly one reservation record (heap + requirement bytes) | `buf->reservation = std::move(reservation)` after every fallible step has succeeded |
+| 6 | That reservation is released exactly once | `vk_heap_reservation::release()` is idempotent (`held` flag); `~vk_buffer_struct` runs it once |
+| 7 | Underflow (double release, release of bytes never reserved) fails closed and leaves the counter **untouched** — no silent clamping | `vk_heap_ledger::release()` returns `false` |
+| 8 | Selection uses reserved + candidate requirement, and with an empty ledger is byte-identical to the old `heap.size >= mem_req.size` test | `ggml_vk_find_memory_properties()` → `vk_heaps().would_fit(...)` |
+| 9 | Two memory types reporting the same `heapIndex` share one counter; separate heaps are independent | ledger keyed by `heapIndex`, fixed `uint64_t[VK_MAX_MEMORY_HEAPS]` array under the lock (no allocation while holding the mutex) |
+| 10 | Accounting output is gated on the existing trace facility | new `reserved=` / `budget=` fields and the `state=refuse_budget` line are inside `if (alloc_trace)`; the bulk-large-heap stderr receipt is untouched |
+
+### Decisions (deliberate, not omissions)
+
+| Decision | Rationale |
+|---|---|
+| Release lives in `~vk_buffer_struct`, not in `ggml_vk_destroy_buffer()` | `ggml_vk_destroy_buffer()` does `buf.reset()` on a *reference*; other `shared_ptr` copies can keep the allocation alive. Releasing there would drop the reservation while the driver still holds the bytes — under-counting the ledger and re-admitting the very over-allocation this closes. `ggml_vk_destroy_buffer()` therefore releases exactly once transitively, when the last reference dies. |
+| The **imported host-pointer path is not charged** against the heap (`held == false`, nothing to release) | An imported pointer is memory the caller already owns and the driver only wraps. Charging it would refuse imports that consume no new heap bytes. Classified explicitly; there is no double reserve and no release on that path. |
+| Budget == the heap's reported size | B7a has no headroom factor and no env knob. Deliberately no config for a value that never varies in this phase. |
+| Heap-index keying is the Vulkan abstraction only | Two types sharing a `heapIndex` share a pool *by Vulkan's own definition*. Nothing here infers a physical channel, bank or link from a logical heap index. |
+| Fallback candidates | Each candidate reserves independently and rolls back before the next is tried, so the R46 bulk-large-heap policy's fall-through to the stock chain cannot leak or double-book. |
+
