+## 5. Red-first evidence
+
+### 5.1 Defect 1 — the guard (covers the ledger seam)
+
+The defect was planted in the **shared** mechanism — `vk_heap_reservation::release()` returned
+success without releasing the ledger bytes — so it breaks `ggml_vk_create_buffer()` and the tests
+identically. A defect in a test-only loop would have proved nothing.
+
+With the defect (`04-heapres-planted-red.txt`, rc **1**):
+
+```
+  FAIL failed candidates left heap 0 at zero
+  FAIL destruction released the reservation
+  FAIL second destruction released nothing more
+  FAIL all candidates failing left the ledger empty
+heapres: FAILURES
+```
+
+Missing rollback and missing release are both caught. The defect was then reverted, rebuilt and
+re-run three times green (rc 0 each).
+
+### 5.2 Defect 2 — the production wiring (covers `ggml_vk_create_buffer` → `~vk_buffer_struct`)
+
+The seam alone cannot prove that a *real* buffer takes and releases a reservation: if
+`buf->reservation = std::move(reservation)` were dropped, all 38 seam assertions would still pass.
+That gap is closed on the real device, with the ledger's own `reserved=` trace field as the probe
+(`r46b-b7a-evidence/ledger_trend.py`, results in `14-trace-differential.txt`):
+
+| Run | decreases | increases | final reserved (per heap) |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Green (`13-…mulmat.txt`) | **25** | 29 | `{0:0, 1:0, 2:13,693,408}`, max on heap 2 was 27,146,720 |
+| Defect 2 (`16-…mulmat-red2.txt`) | **0** | 1708 | `{0:15,260,877,796, 1:287,492,608, 2:268,397,444}` — monotonic climb, 15 GB never released |
+| Green after revert (`19-…mulmat-green2.txt`) | **25** | 29 | identical to the first green run |
+
+With the hand-off removed the ledger never decreases once; with it in place the ledger falls 25 times
+during the run and ends well below its maximum. That is destruction releasing reservations through
+the real allocator, not through the test seam.
+
+Both defects were reverted. Post-revert `git status` shows exactly three modified files, and the
+green MUL_MAT trend reproduced byte-identically. The hashes in §2 are of the shipping sources, which
+were rebuilt and re-run green as command 14.
+
+Note on the two counters: the pre-existing trace `heap_live` and this ledger deliberately do **not**
+agree. `heap_live` drops in `ggml_vk_destroy_buffer` and never sees a buffer freed by plain
+`shared_ptr` death; the ledger drops in `~vk_buffer_struct` and never counts an imported host pointer
+(hence heap 1 at 0 reserved against ~1.19 GB of live host imports). The equality check is therefore
+not the right assertion — the trend check above is, and it is the one that discriminates.
+
+## 6. Owed — NOT done in B7a
+
+- **B7b — whole-model allocation planning.** Nothing here plans a model's total footprint ahead of
+  the first buffer. The ledger is reactive, admitting one buffer at a time; a load can still fail
+  late, just no longer because two buffers were admitted against the same bytes.
+- **B7c — host splitting and staging reserve.** No staging-buffer reserve, no host/device split
+  policy, no spill planning.
+- Budget headroom / configurability (driver overhead, fragmentation, `VK_EXT_memory_budget`
+  `heapBudget` instead of `heapSize`) is not modelled.
+
+## 7. Material limitations
+
+1. **Pre-existing leak window, not fixed here.** If bind/map/BDA setup throws after `allocateMemory`
+   succeeds, `buf->size` is still 0, so the existing `~vk_buffer_struct` early-returns and the
+   **Vulkan allocation leaks**. The function-scope guard correctly rolls the *reservation* back, so
+   in that window the ledger and the driver diverge (ledger optimistic). This is a pre-existing bug
+   in the destructor and was deliberately left alone — fixing it is out of B7a scope.
+2. The ledger tracks **this process only**. Another process or another Vulkan device instance
+   consuming the same physical heap is invisible to it.
+3. Budget is the heap's reported `size`, which ignores driver overhead and fragmentation, so a
+   reservation that the ledger admits can still be refused by the driver. That path is handled
+   (rollback + next candidate), but it means the ledger is a necessary, not sufficient, condition.
+4. Imported host pointers are uncharged by design (§1). If a future path imports memory that *does*
+   consume fresh heap bytes, that classification must be revisited.
+5. `would_fit()` in selection is advisory; `reserve()` is the authority. A racing reservation between
+   the two simply refuses the candidate at reserve time and moves to the next one.
+6. Device-side proof is bounded: the real-device evidence is one narrow correctness op (`ADD`,
+   `-b Vulkan0`). No model load, no inference, no timing runs — those are outside the fences.
+7. `heapres` is registered as a ctest only when `GGML_VULKAN` is on; on a non-Vulkan build the mode
+   and its externs compile out entirely. `GGML_USE_VULKAN` is added **PRIVATE** to the
+   `test-backend-ops` target only; `grep -rn GGML_USE_VULKAN ggml/include/ common/ src/` returns **0
+   hits**, so no header gates a type, inline function or enum on it and there is no ODR/ABI hazard
+   from defining it for that one TU.
+8. **Shared build tree side effect.** `cmake -S . -B build-vulkan` re-ran the `vulkan-shaders-gen`
+   external project, which invalidated every generated shader and forced a 666-target rebuild of the
+   shared `build-vulkan` tree. It ended green and the tree is consistent, but other sessions using
+   that tree paid a full rebuild.
+9. `vk_heaps()` is a function-local static, so `~vk_buffer_struct` can touch it during static
+   teardown. This is the same pattern as the pre-existing `vk_alloc_trace()` mutex — inherited
+   exposure, not introduced here.
+10. The test seam (`ggml_vk_heapres_*`, including `ggml_vk_heapres_reset()`, which can zero the
+    production ledger) is linked into every `GGML_VULKAN` build. That is the accepted cost of the
+    prompt's "narrowly added test mode"; the symbols are internal and in no public header.
+11. Trace `heap_live` and the ledger count the same bytes independently (§5.2). Left in place on
+    purpose — the divergence is what discriminates the wiring — and marked with a `ponytail:` comment
+    naming the upgrade path.
+
