+# R46 B7a R2 — repair of the four blocking defects
+
+- Worktree: `C:/ArifiLabs/cache/r46b-wt`
+- Branch: `lane-230/r46b-rebased-2026-09-11`
+- Inherited HEAD: `ae831efde` — unchanged. Nothing was fetched, pulled, merged, rebased, committed,
+  pushed or tagged. All work is in the working tree.
+- Date: 2026-09-13
+- Scope: the same three files as R1. No fourth file was needed.
+
+## 1. The four findings and how each was closed
+
+### Finding 1 — post-allocation exception must free the VkDeviceMemory exactly once
+
+Root fix, not a patch on the reported path. A function-scope RAII owner,
+`vk_buffer_setup_guard` (`ggml-vulkan.cpp:1666-1707`), takes ownership of `buf->buffer` the moment
+`createBuffer()` returns and of `buf->device_memory` the moment `allocateMemory()` returns. It is the
+**sole** owner until `setup_guard.commit()`, which is the last statement before `return buf`.
+
+Every failure in that window — the two import-path `return {}` sites, the candidate-loop rethrow, the
+"no suitable memory type" throw, and now also a throw from `mapMemory` / `bindBufferMemory` /
+`getBufferAddress` / `log_allocation` — frees whatever the guard is holding at that point (at the two import-path sites that is the VkBuffer
+only; from the candidate loop onward it is the VkBuffer and the VkDeviceMemory).
+
+Exactly-once is by construction, not by an invariant on `buf->size`:
+
+- the guard **clears** `buf->device_memory`, `buf->buffer`, `buf->ptr` and `buf->size` as it destroys
+  them, so `~vk_buffer_struct()` can never reach a freed handle;
+- the four hand-written `device->device.destroyBuffer(buf->buffer)` calls inside
+  `ggml_vk_create_buffer()` were **removed**, so there is exactly one cleanup site in the function;
+- `commit()` is an explicit ownership transfer, so the success path frees exactly once, from the
+  buffer destructor.
+
+Destruction order is stated and commented: `vk_heap_reservation reservation` is declared **before**
+`vk_buffer_setup_guard setup_guard`, so on unwind the guard runs first (driver memory really freed)
+and the ledger is told the bytes are free only afterwards. The ledger can never report free bytes
+the driver still holds.
+
+### Finding 2 — no production-linked reset of a live ledger
+
+Two independent closures:
+
+1. **Compile isolation.** The entire seam is inside `#ifdef GGML_VK_INTERNAL_TESTS`, and that
+   definition is added to the `ggml-vulkan` target **only from `tests/CMakeLists.txt`**, i.e. only
+   when `LLAMA_BUILD_TESTS` is on and the `tests` subdirectory is added. `vk_heap_ledger::reset()`
+   and `total_reserved()` are themselves inside the same `#ifdef`, so a production build has no
+   reset entry point at all — not an exported one, not a member one. The fault-injection macro
+   compiles to `do { } while (0)` in that build.
+2. **No shared state even in the test build.** The arithmetic half of the seam now operates on
+   `vk_heapres_test_ledger()`, a ledger instance **owned by the test**. There is deliberately no
+   entry point that can reset, or even reach, a device's ledger. A test can no longer corrupt an
+   active allocation because it has nothing to corrupt.
+
+Proved empirically, not asserted: `ggml-vulkan.cpp` was recompiled with the identical ninja command
+minus `-DGGML_VK_INTERNAL_TESTS`, and the resulting object contains **0** `heapres` / `vk_test_fault`
+symbols, against 10 exported `ggml_vk_heapres_*` symbols in the test-enabled object
+(`r46b-b7a-r2-evidence/08-seam-absent-in-production.txt`).
+
+### Finding 3 — accounting keyed by device identity, not heap index alone
+
+Root fix: the ledger is no longer a process singleton. `vk_heaps()` was **deleted**. `vk_heap_ledger`
+is now a **member of `vk_device_struct`** (`ggml-vulkan.cpp:1103`), so:
+
+- two Vulkan devices that both report heap 0/1 have two separate counters and two separate budgets;
+- device identity cannot alias stale state, because the ledger's lifetime *is* the device's lifetime.
+  There is no handle, index or pointer used as a key that a destroyed-and-recreated device could
+  collide with;
+- `vk_heap_reservation` carries a `vk_heap_ledger *` naming the ledger it reserved from, so a
+  reservation taken on device A can only ever be released back to device A;
+- `ggml_vk_find_memory_properties()` takes the device's ledger as a parameter instead of consulting
+  a global.
+
+Lifetime is handled explicitly: `vk_buffer_struct::reservation` is now declared **after**
+`vk_device device`, and `~vk_buffer_struct()` calls `reservation.release()` unconditionally as its
+first statement, above the `size == 0` early return. The explicit release is the guarantee; the
+declaration order is the backstop if an early return is added later.
+
+### Finding 4 — negative tests through production ownership and wiring
+
+`test-backend-ops heapres` now has a second half, `heapres_fault_tests()`, that drives the **real**
+`ggml_vk_create_buffer()` on a **real** device with a fault injected at four production points:
+
+| Stage | Injection point | Guard state exercised |
+|---|---|---|
+| 1 | after `allocateMemory` succeeded | memory only |
+| 2 | after `mapMemory` | memory + mapped |
+| 3 | after `bindBufferMemory` | memory + mapped + bound |
+| 4 | after `getBufferAddress` | fully set up, `buf->size` already assigned — so this one also proves the guard cannot double-free against `~vk_buffer_struct()` |
+
+For each stage the test asserts that `create_buffer` threw and that the device ledger returned
+**exactly** to its pre-call value. A stage-0 (no fault) probe runs first and asserts `mapped == 1`,
