+- Evidence: `r46b-b7a-r3-evidence/` (566 KB). Every executed build, test and planted-red command has
+  a `.rc` receipt in the uniform `rc=<n>` format, captured with `cmd > file 2>&1; echo "rc=$?" >
+  file.rc` — no pipeline, so no status loss. The static, read-only inspection commands (rows 11, 15,
+  20 and 21: the SHA-256 revert-identity compare, the `nm` symbol census and the two `grep -n` source
+  censuses) have no separate `.rc` file; their rc column is an em dash and only their captured output
+  is evidence.
+
+## 1. Finding 1 — destruction must not release capacity before the driver memory is freed
+
+### The defect
+
+`~vk_buffer_struct()` (R2) called `reservation.release()` as its **first** statement and only then
+called `vkFreeMemory` / `vkDestroyBuffer`. In that window the ledger advertised the bytes as free
+while the driver still owned them, so a concurrent `ggml_vk_create_buffer()` could be admitted
+against bytes that were still in use — the same ledger/driver divergence the setup guard prevents on
+the failure path.
+
+### The fix (`ggml-vulkan.cpp`, `~vk_buffer_struct()`)
+
+Driver first, ledger last:
+
+```cpp
+if (size != 0) {
+    VK_LOG_DEBUG(...);
+    VK_TEST_NOTE_DRIVER_FREE(device);
+    device->device.freeMemory(device_memory);
+    device->device.destroyBuffer(buffer);
+}
+// unconditional and last
+reservation.release();
+```
+
+The R2 properties that put `release()` first are kept: the call is still **unconditional** (it runs
+on the `size == 0` path too, so it does not depend on `size` being initialized) and still idempotent
+(`release()` returns false when `held` is false), so exact-once and no-underflow are unchanged. The
+`size != 0` early **return** was replaced by a `size != 0` **block**, which is what lets the release
+stay unconditional while moving below the frees. `VK_LOG_DEBUG` stays inside that block.
+
+### Why this is concurrency-safe without a lock
+
+Releasing last makes the ledger transiently **over**-count: for the duration of `vkFreeMemory` the
+ledger still charges bytes the driver has already taken back. That is the conservative direction —
+it can refuse an allocation a fraction early, it can never admit an overcommit. The former order was
+the dangerous direction. The release itself remains mutex-protected inside `vk_heap_ledger`, and the
+reservation's `held` flag is only ever touched by the owning buffer, so no additional lock is needed
+and none was added.
+
+### Every release site was audited, not just the reported line
+
+| Site | Order | Verdict |
+|---|---|---|
+| `~vk_buffer_struct()` body (`:1678-1695`) | driver free → release | **fixed here** |
+| `~vk_heap_reservation()` (`:1649`) | runs after the body's explicit release | no-op, correct |
+| `ggml_vk_create_buffer()` function-scope `reservation` (`:4327`) | declared **before** `vk_buffer_setup_guard`, so on unwind the guard frees the driver objects first and the reservation releases after | already correct |
+| candidate-loop `catch` (`:4497`) | `allocateMemory` threw, so no driver memory exists to free | correct by construction |
+| `vk_heap_reservation::operator=(&&)` (`:1617`) | releases the *target*, which is the empty `buf->reservation` at the `std::move` handoff (`:4564`) | no-op, correct |
+| test-seam `first_fit` (`:23037`) | test ledger, no driver objects | not a production path |
+
+`freeMemory` / `destroyBuffer` appear at exactly **two** live sites in the file (`:1687-1688`
+destructor, `:1738-1742` guard; the other two hits are comments at `:4355` and `:9735`) — census
+receipt `24-free-sites.txt`. Both now free the driver objects before any ledger capacity is returned.
+The release-site table above is backed by `25-release-sites.txt`.
+
+### The ordering is proved by a witness, not by inspection
+
+`after == before` cannot distinguish the two orders. `VK_TEST_NOTE_DRIVER_FREE(device)` samples
+`device->heap_ledger.total_reserved()` at the exact instant `vkFreeMemory` is called, and
+`ggml_vk_heapres_destroy_order_probe()` creates one real buffer through the production allocator,
+arms the witness across the single `buf.reset()`, and disarms it immediately. Arming is scoped
+because device/instance teardown destroys buffers of its own that would otherwise clobber the
+sample. Five assertions:
+
+```
+OK   destroy order: probe ran
+OK   destroy order: a live buffer holds its bytes in the ledger
+OK   destroy order: ledger still counted the bytes when vkFreeMemory was called
+OK   destroy order: capacity was NOT released before the driver memory was freed
+OK   destroy order: released exactly once afterwards
+```
+
+Planted **red C** (§4) restores the R2 order and turns the third and fourth of those into `FAIL`
+with process rc **1**. The test therefore discriminates on order, which was the checker's objection.
+
+## 2. Finding 2 — BDA coverage must prove `getBufferAddress` actually executed
+
+### The defect
+
+`VK_TEST_FAULT(VK_TEST_FAULT_AFTER_BDA)` fires **after** the `if (device->buffer_device_address)`
+block whether or not that block ran. On a device without BDA the suite still printed
+`fault after getBufferAddress: ...  OK`, claiming coverage of a call that never happened.
+
+### The fix
+
+1. `VK_TEST_NOTE_BDA()` is placed **inside** the `if (device->buffer_device_address)` block,
+   immediately after `device->device.getBufferAddress(addressInfo)` — so the witness can only be set
+   by the real call on the real path.
+2. `ggml_vk_heapres_fault_probe()` gained two out-params: `bda_supported`
+   (`device->buffer_device_address`, read from the live device) and `bda_executed` (the witness).
+3. The test claims BDA coverage **only** against the witness:
+   - `bda_supported == 1` → assert `bda_executed == 1` at stage 0 **and** inside the stage-4 run.
+   - `bda_supported == 0` → print an explicit `UNAVAILABLE:` line saying `getBufferAddress` never
+     runs on this device and that stage 4 therefore covers post-`buf->size` cleanup only.
+
+Stage 4 is **not** skipped when BDA is unavailable. It carries two distinct claims, and only one of
+them is about BDA: the other — that the guard and `~vk_buffer_struct()` cannot both free once
+`buf->size` is set — is a preserved R2 property and is provable with no BDA at all. Only the
+BDA-named assertion is gated.
+
+### This rig — CHECKED, not assumed
+
+`ggml_vk_heapres_fault_probe` reports `bda_supported == 1` on Vulkan0 (Radeon 780M), and the witness
+fires. Runtime BDA coverage **is** available here and was exercised
+(`r46b-b7a-r3-evidence/13-heapres-final-r1.txt`):
+
+```
+OK   no fault: device reports bufferDeviceAddress AND getBufferAddress really executed
+OK   fault after getBufferAddress: getBufferAddress really executed before the fault
+```
+
+Planted **red D** (§4) deletes `VK_TEST_NOTE_BDA()` and both assertions go `FAIL` at rc **1**, so the
+witness is not itself green-but-blind. The `UNAVAILABLE` branch is unreachable on this rig and is
+therefore **not** covered by a runtime receipt; it is a printf-only path with no assertion.
